# hooks.env — per-repo config for the fail-closed gate hooks (scripts/lint-format.sh,
# scripts/arch-fitness.sh). Uncomment the block for your stack; nothing else is needed.
#
# The framework ships this file REAL but INERT (every line commented out) — the same mechanism as
# `guard-generated.globs`, so a consumer that drops `build/hooks/` at `.claude/hooks/` gets it for
# free with no extra copy step, and each gate is an informative no-op until a line is uncommented.
#
# PARSED, NOT SOURCED — but this file is still CODE, not inert data. The four values below are
# `bash -c`'d by the gates (that is what they are for), automatically on every `subagentStop` with no
# tool-approval prompt in between. So anything that can write this file can run commands on the
# workstation. Parsing rather than sourcing removes *incidental* execution — a stray line, an `$(…)`
# in a value, anything outside the four known keys — and shrinks the shape from "arbitrary shell
# anywhere in the file" to "one command in one of four values". It does not make the file safe;
# `.claude/hooks/*` is an active glob in guard-generated.globs for the same reason (which gates an
# agent's Write/Edit, not a shell redirect — see that file's RESIDUAL note).
#
# Precedence is FIRST WINS and is announced in the gate output: a value already exported in your shell
# or CI beats the file, and an earlier line beats a later duplicate — so uncommenting BOTH stack blocks
# below gets you the first one, with a message saying the second was ignored. Quote values that carry
# a trailing `# comment`. A line with a known key but no `=` is reported and skipped, not run.

# ── Backend (Gradle) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Check FIRST whether your build CI already runs the ArchUnit fitness rules as part of `test`
# (e.g. `./gradlew clean build` depends on every subproject's Test task). If it does, leave this
# unset: SubagentStop gates are advisory (exit 1 is reported, not enforced — README "Gate hooks"),
# and arming it costs a full compile-and-run at a 600s budget on every code-writing subagent stop
# for an answer CI already gives. Use a fully qualified task so Gradle does not configure every
# project looking for it. `--offline` is deliberately absent — see the note at the bottom.
# ARCH_FITNESS_CMD="./gradlew :application:archTest"
#
# LINT_FIX_CMD / LINT_CHECK_CMD: set them only once a formatter plugin (spotless, checkstyle, …) is
# applied to the build, and re-wire lint-format.sh in .claude/settings.json in the SAME change.
# A gate registered with no command exits 0 on every run and buys false confidence.
# LINT_FIX_CMD="./gradlew spotlessApply"         # formatter auto-fix
# LINT_CHECK_CMD="./gradlew spotlessCheck"       # formatter verify

# ── Frontend (Node) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ARCH_FITNESS_CMD="npx depcruise src"           # dependency-cruiser
# LINT_FIX_CMD="npm run format"                  # prettier / eslint --fix
# LINT_CHECK_CMD="npm run lint"                  # eslint / prettier --check

# ── verify-claims (self-report verification) ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Neither value is ever executed — unlike the three *_CMD keys above, these are a git ref and a file
# path. verify-claims.sh works with both unset; setting them only makes it stricter.
#
# VERIFY_CLAIMS_BASE     the ref a part branch forks from, used for the COMMITTED half of the
#                        changed-file set. Unset → derived from the branch name
#                        (`feature/<ticket>/part-<layer>-<cap>` → the shared `feature/<ticket>-*`
#                        branch), then origin/HEAD, then origin/main. Set it only if this repo's
#                        part branches fork from something else.
# VERIFY_CLAIMS_DECLARED path to the part's plan file. Its `- Declared Files:` field is read via
#                        scripts/plan-slice.sh and at least one changed path must fall inside it.
#                        Unset → the gate still fails an agent that changed NOTHING, but cannot tell
#                        whether real changes landed in the right place, and says so on every run.
# VERIFY_CLAIMS_BASE="origin/main"
# VERIFY_CLAIMS_DECLARED="docs/<epic>/<ticket>/domain.md"

# ── Which roles the gates apply to ────────────────────────────────────────────
# `subagentStop` fires for EVERY subagent, so the gates filter on the payload's `agentName` and run
# only for roles that write code. Default: "developer test-writer contract-agent". Override to widen
# or narrow it; an unrecognised/absent `agentName` always RUNS the gate (fail closed).
# GATE_AGENTS="developer test-writer contract-agent"

# ── On `--offline` ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The examples above deliberately omit `--offline`. `./gradlew … --offline` hard-fails on a fresh
# clone or after a dependency bump (cold cache), and lint-format.sh treats a non-zero LINT_FIX_CMD as
# "the formatter failed to RUN" and bounces the part — for a reason that has nothing to do with the
# code. Add `--offline` only where you know the Gradle cache is always warm.
